{"cuisine/fusion-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/fusion-cuisine","n":"Fusion cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["Fusion cuisine is a cuisine that combines elements of different culinary traditions that originate from different countries, regions, or cultures. Cuisines of this type are not categorized according to any one particular cuisine style and have played a part in many contemporary restaurant cuisines since the 1970s."],["The term fusion cuisine, added to the Oxford English Dictionary in 2002, is defined as \"a style of cookery which blends ingredients and methods of preparation from different countries, regions, or ethnic groups; food cooked in this style.\""]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"fusion-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fusion_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Karen Keygnaert","dish/karen-keygnaert"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Fusion cooking","count":16,"links":[["American Jewish cuisine","cuisine/american-jewish-cuisine"],["Cajun cuisine","cuisine/cajun-cuisine"],["Chinese American cuisine","cuisine/chinese-american-cuisine"],["Cuisine of the Pennsylvania Dutch","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-pennsylvania-dutch"],["Filipino-American cuisine","cuisine/filipino-american-cuisine"],["Floribbean cuisine","cuisine/floribbean-cuisine"],["German-American cuisine","cuisine/german-americans-cuisine"],["Greek-American cuisine","cuisine/greek-american-cuisine"],["Indian-American cuisine","cuisine/indian-american-cuisine"],["Italian-American cuisine","cuisine/italian-american-cuisine"],["Japanese-American cuisine","cuisine/japanese-american-cuisine"],["Korean-American cuisine","cuisine/korean-americans-cuisine"],["Mexican-American cuisine","cuisine/mexican-american-cuisine"],["New American cuisine","cuisine/new-american-cuisine"],["Thai-American cuisine","cuisine/thai-american-cuisine"],["Vietnamese-American cuisine","cuisine/vietnamese-americans-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/0e/Minchi_e_bacalhaus.jpg/500px-Minchi_e_bacalhaus.jpg","w":500,"h":374,"by":"K.Y.K.Z.K.","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minchi_e_bacalhaus.jpg"}},"cuisine/chettinad-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/chettinad-cuisine","n":"Chettinad cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Tamil cuisine","cuisine/tamil-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Chettinadu cuisine is the cuisine of a community called the Nagarathars, from the  Chettinad region in Sivaganga district of Tamil Nadu state in"],["India. Chettinad cuisine is perhaps the most renowned fare in the Tamil Nadu repertoire. It uses a variety of spices and the dishes are made with fresh ground masalas. Chettiars also use a variety of sun-dried meats and salted vegetables, reflecting the dry environment of the region. Most of the dishes are eaten with rice and rice based accompaniments such as  dosas, appams, idiyappams, adais and idlis. The Chettiars, through their mercantile contacts with Burma, learnt to prepare a type of rice pudding made with sticky red rice. The chefs of Manapatti village near Singampunari are experts in cooking Chettinad cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"chettinad-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chettinad_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Archana's Kitchen (Oriya)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"archanaskitchen.com","note":"Chettinad-tagged recipes on Archana Doshi's large Indian recipe site.","url":"https://www.archanaskitchen.com/tag/chettinad-recipes/page-2","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Bangala Table: Flavors and Recipes from Chettinad","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Sumeet Nair and Meenakshi Meyyappan","note":"Chettinad recipes from The Bangala heritage hotel in Karaikudi.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Tamil cuisine","cuisine/tamil-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c6/ChickenChettinad.JPG/500px-ChickenChettinad.JPG","w":500,"h":749,"by":"Maccess Corporation","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ChickenChettinad.JPG"}},"dish/black-pudding":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/black-pudding","n":"Black pudding","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Black pudding is a type of blood sausage originating in the United Kingdom and Ireland. It is made from pork or occasionally beef blood, with pork fat or beef suet, and a cereal, usually oatmeal, oat groats, or barley groats. The high proportion of cereal, along with the use of certain herbs, distinguishes black pudding from other blood sausages."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-black-pudding"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_pudding"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"],["Irish cuisine","cuisine/irish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Blood as food","ingredient/blood-as-food"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Suet","ingredient/suet"],["Tyrolean Speck","ingredient/tyrolean-speck"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Great British Chefs: traditional British recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"greatbritishchefs.com","